Narrow host-side geometry test for the corrupt-save notice's Continue button
(parent, msg 3946/3948): replaces the killed test/corrupt_notice_touch/
full-pipeline harness ("four stub-gaps deep"). Tests
GameRenderer::hitTestCorruptSaveNoticeContinue() directly -- a public
function, pure geometry, no display, no Bitmap, no i18n codegen, no HalFile.

This compiles the REAL, unmodified GameRenderer.cpp/UITheme.cpp (plus the
four real, unmodified theme .cpp files their static-instance construction
transitively touches) against fast stub shadows of every heavy dependency
(GfxRenderer, HalDisplay, EpdFontFamily, I18n/StrId, GameState) -- same
"shadow header/.cpp, real .cpp never actually reaches it at runtime"
technique as test/whole_run_corrupt_save/, adapted for a render/UI target
instead of an activity/control-flow target.

Not a gtest suite (see test/README for those).

## Why GameRenderer::initForTest() is enough

GameRenderer::initForTest(screenW, screenH) is a small, public, test-only
entry point (GameRenderer.h) that sets screenW/screenH and calls the
private computeLayout() -- no GfxRenderer instance, no HAL, nothing
display-related. hitTestCorruptSaveNoticeContinue() is likewise public,
pure geometry over screenW/screenH -- the same entry point
GameActivity::loop()'s real touch dispatch calls in production. Neither
function touches GfxRenderer, HalDisplay, or I18n at all; those only need
to exist because GameRenderer.cpp is one translation unit and its other
(unreached, by this harness) methods reference them -- the linker needs
every symbol to resolve even though this harness's main() never calls
those other methods.

## Why the button's rect is discovered by scanning, not read directly

corruptNoticeContinueRect() is private -- the harness never reads it.
Instead CorruptNoticeHitTestHarness.cpp scans hitTestCorruptSaveNoticeContinue(x, y)
across the full screen to discover the hit region's bounds, then asserts a
point at the discovered center is a hit and a point above the discovered
top edge is not -- proving the button responds to touch without hardcoding
any pixel geometry that could silently drift out of sync with the real
layout code.

## Build

  INCLUDES="-I test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stubs \
    -I src -I src/game -I src/components \
    -I lib/GfxRenderer -I lib/hal -I lib/I18n"

  mkdir -p test/corrupt_notice_hittest/build
  for f in \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/CorruptNoticeHitTestHarness.cpp \
      src/game/GameRenderer.cpp \
      src/components/UITheme.cpp \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stub_themes/BaseTheme.cpp \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stub_themes/LyraTheme.cpp \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stub_themes/RoundedRaffTheme.cpp \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stub_themes/Lyra3CoversTheme.cpp \
      test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stub_gamestate/GameState.cpp \
      src/game/GameSprites.cpp; do
    g++ -std=c++20 -O0 -Wall -Wextra $INCLUDES -c "$f" \
      -o "test/corrupt_notice_hittest/build/$(basename $f .cpp).o"
  done
  g++ test/corrupt_notice_hittest/build/*.o \
    -o test/corrupt_notice_hittest/build/corrupt_notice_hittest_harness

  ./test/corrupt_notice_hittest/build/corrupt_notice_hittest_harness

NOTE: all four theme .cpp files (BaseTheme/LyraTheme/RoundedRaffTheme/
Lyra3CoversTheme) are the inert stub_themes/ versions (real headers, empty
out-of-line method bodies), not the real .cpp files. An earlier draft of
this README used the REAL BaseTheme.cpp, on the theory that only its
constructor/vtable needed to link -- that turned out wrong in practice:
BaseTheme.cpp's own body (not just its transitive header includes) pulls
in HalClock.h -> Arduino.h and constructs real Bitmap objects from file
paths, both explicitly out of scope per parent's msg 3946/3948 ("no
display, no Bitmap"). The stub version's empty out-of-line bodies satisfy
the Itanium ABI key-function/vtable-linking requirement without any of
that -- confirmed sufficient because this harness's tested path
(GameRenderer::initForTest() + hitTestCorruptSaveNoticeContinue()) never
actually invokes any BaseTheme paint method at runtime; the linker only
needs every symbol GameRenderer.cpp/UITheme.cpp reference to resolve, not
for real ones to be reachable. All four stub themes link because
UITheme::instance's constructor (UITheme.cpp) can construct any of the
four depending on SETTINGS.uiTheme -- stubs/CrossPointSettings.h's
uiTheme default (LYRA_3_COVERS, matching the real class's own default)
determines which one is actually constructed at runtime, but since all
four are equally inert stubs it doesn't matter which for this harness.

## Notable stub-placement gotcha (same class as test/whole_run_corrupt_save/)

GameRenderer.h does `#include <GfxRenderer.h>` (angle-bracket) so the stub
must be reachable via a flat -I stub dir (test/corrupt_notice_hittest/stubs/GfxRenderer.h)
-- confirmed correct. UITheme.cpp does `"components/themes/BaseTheme.h"` etc.
(quoted, subdir-qualified) -- these are REAL headers, included via -I src
resolving src/components/themes/BaseTheme.h directly; no stub needed for
the .h, only for BaseTheme.cpp's sibling theme .cpp files where a stub is
used.

## i18n codegen gap (parent, msg 3946 -- logged separately)

lib/I18n/I18nKeys.h is gitignored and generated by scripts/gen_i18n.py --
never present in a clean checkout. This harness's stubs/I18n.h is a
hand-written shadow covering only the 20 StrId keys GameRenderer.cpp
actually references (confirmed via grep), not a substitute for real
codegen. See /workspace/agent/crosspoint_backlog.md for the tracked
follow-up item.

## Output

PASS prints all assertions passed (including the discovered hit-region
bounds) and exits 0; any failed CHECK() prints to stderr, the run still
completes so all checks are reported, and main() exits 1.
